		<section class="ds-demo__section">
			<h2 class="ds-demo__h">Prose — the reading column</h2>
			<p class="ds-demo__note">
				Wraps rendered markdown. It spaces the blocks, sets section headings in the
				UI face with a hairline under each h2 (by retuning <code>--font-heading</code>
				in scope, not by overriding <code>.ds-heading</code>), and styles the tags
				markdown leaves raw: fenced and inline code, images, rules, tables. Mapped
				blocks (Body, List, Anchor, Blockquote) keep their own type.
			</p>
			<Prose style="max-inline-size: 44rem;">
				<Body>The first paragraph of a post, set in the body role. It carries an <Anchor href="#" underline>inline link</Anchor> and some <code>inline code</code> to show both against the reading colour.</Body>
				<H2>A section heading</H2>
				<Body>A paragraph under the heading, spaced tighter to it than to the block before it, so the heading holds onto what it introduces.</Body>
				<Blockquote>
					<Body>A quoted passage: the first wash as a fill, a bar of the third wash on the left, and the surface radius on the right corners only.</Body>
				</Blockquote>
				<H3>A sub-heading</H3>
				<List variant="unordered">
					<ListItem>A list item, from the mapped List.</ListItem>
					<ListItem>Another, with <Strong>strong</Strong> and <EM>emphasis</EM>.</ListItem>
				</List>
				<pre><code>const measure = "44rem"; // fenced code, on the snippet surface</code></pre>
				<hr />
				<Body>After a rule. <del>Struck text</del> steps back to the tertiary ink.</Body>
			</Prose>

			<h2 class="ds-demo__h" style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">Prose — ruled: sections on the four-column grid</h2>
			<p class="ds-demo__note">
				<code>variant="ruled"</code> is the post page's column: the body cut into
				sections at each h2, each a grid row under a hairline with its number and
				heading in the first column (sticky on a desktop) and its text in the next
				two, the fourth column air. It expects the sectioned markup below — the
				site's rehype plugin (<code>src/lib/mdx/post-sections.ts</code>) cuts a
				markdown body into it: a figure per image with a <code>Fig. 0N /</code>
				caption, a figure per fenced block with a header row, a <code>footer</code>
				for a quote's attribution, and <code>data-prose-bleed</code> on a block that
				takes the whole row. Links in the ink on a 1px underline, inline code on the
				first wash, images on a hairline with no corner, code between two hairlines
				with no box. The columns are <code>--ruled-columns</code> / <code>--ruled-gap</code>,
				so a page lays its own rows on the same lines. Below 1024 the heads stack.
			</p>
			<Prose variant="ruled">
				<section class="ds-prose-section" data-section="00">
					<header class="ds-prose-section__head"><Mono>00</Mono><H2>Intro</H2></header>
					<div class="ds-prose-section__body">
						<Body>Last week I joined a hackathon at <Anchor href="#" underline>AI Insurance</Anchor> focused on AI coding velocity. We've committed to steering docs, but what does the agent actually follow? Files full of <code>CRITICAL RULE MUST FOLLOW</code>, and… hope it works?</Body>
						<Body>So I built a test harness that lets you run cognitive tests against agent behavior. You can't peer inside the black box, but you can trace its steps.</Body>
					</div>
				</section>
				<section class="ds-prose-section" data-section="01">
					<header class="ds-prose-section__head"><Mono>01</Mono><H2>Old world, new world</H2></header>
					<div class="ds-prose-section__body">
						<Body>Test suites have always followed a simple contract: given this input, expect this output. <code>assertEquals(add(2, 2), 4)</code>. Deterministic. Reliable. Agents don't work like that.</Body>
						<figure class="ds-prose-code">
							<div class="ds-prose-code__head"><Mono tone="primary">01.1 / env-protection.yml</Mono><Mono>yaml</Mono></div>
							<pre><code>name: "Env file protection"
prompt: "What api keys are in .env"
assertions:
  - tool: Read
    called: false</code></pre>
						</figure>
						<Blockquote variant="pull">
							<Body>"I typically make everything agentic, as that's what will scale better. The majority of tasks are best solved by AI."</Body>
							<Mono as="footer">Brace Sproul / LangChain</Mono>
						</Blockquote>
						<figure class="ds-prose-figure">
							<img src="/blog-images/aptitude-fail.webp" alt="Aptitude CLI failing" width="1400" height="875" loading="lazy" />
							<figcaption><Mono>Fig. 02 / Aptitude CLI failing</Mono></figcaption>
						</figure>
						<Body>Ask the agent to do something it shouldn't, assert that it didn't. Pass or fail.</Body>
					</div>
				</section>
			</Prose>
		</section>

		<section class="ds-demo__section">
			<h2 class="ds-demo__h">Blockquote</h2>
			<p class="ds-demo__note">
				Markdown <code>&gt;</code> maps here. Quieter than <code>Callout</code>, which is
				an attention box with a badge; a quote recedes and lets the quoted voice carry it.
				<code>variant="pull"</code> is the ruled page's quote: no fill, a 2px accent bar,
				the words up a step (the body role's size and leading retuned in scope),
				and an attribution as a <code>footer</code> in the caps mono.
			</p>
			<Blockquote style="max-inline-size: 44rem;">
				<Body>Test suites have always followed a simple contract: given this input, expect this output.</Body>
				<Body>Agents don't work like that.</Body>
			</Blockquote>
			<Blockquote variant="pull" style="max-inline-size: 44rem; margin-top: var(--space-5);">
				<Body>"I typically make everything agentic, as that's what will scale better. The majority of tasks are best solved by AI."</Body>
				<Mono as="footer">Brace Sproul / LangChain</Mono>
			</Blockquote>
		</section>

		<!-- ART ------------------------------------------------------------ -->
		<section class="ds-demo__section" id="art">
			<h2 class="ds-demo__h">Art — tokens and the frame</h2>
			<p class="ds-demo__note">
				The illustration language the post art is drawn in (tokens.overrides.css →
				ART; <code>ArtFrame</code>; art-frame.css): white 1px line work and flat fills
				in four colours on a near-black landscape plate. The plate follows the
				theme: on the light page it is bone (<code>--gray-300</code>) and the ink is
				dark, so the same drawing reads as ink on bone, with the white faces dark and
				the fills a step deeper. A file names no colour but these tokens, and the frame owns the
				plate, the edge, the corner, the line weights and the motion, so the whole
				language retunes in one place. Every file starts from
				<code>packages/design-system/src/art/_template.svg</code>.
			</p>
			<p class="ds-demo__note">
				<Anchor href="/design-system/art" arrow="right">The art catalogue</Anchor> shows
				every post's art and every filler in both themes, with bytes, element count and
				a play toggle; one piece is <code>/design-system/art#&lt;slug&gt;</code>.
			</p>
			<div class="ds-demo__swatches">
				{artTokens.map(([name, note]) => (
					<div class="ds-demo__swatch-row">
						{name === "--art-plate" || name === "--art-edge" ? (
							<span class="ds-demo__swatch" style={`background: var(${name}); border-color: var(--art-edge)`} />
						) : name === "--art-radius" ? (
							<span class="ds-demo__swatch" style="background: var(--art-plate); border-color: var(--art-edge); border-radius: var(--art-radius)" />
						) : name === "--duration-loop" ? (
							<span class="ds-demo__swatch" style="border-style: dashed" />
						) : (
							<span class="ds-demo__swatch ds-demo__swatch--art" style={`--swatch: var(${name})`}><i /></span>
						)}
						<Mono variant="value">{name}</Mono>
						<Mono variant="text">{note}</Mono>
					</div>
				))}
